## Stock binding and mass/ODT accounting

Inventory SHA-256: 331f233274960c9e53586c61cafae7bd85ec4f480c835430c72904a9406dc06f. The design JSON binds all rows to current physical-stock authority IDs and source references. Historical runtime display labels can be misleading: the selected Carrot Seed, Cedramber and Cashmeran principal records are NEAT, despite legacy display strings mentioning dilution. Do not substitute a real dilute bottle for these selected neat rows.

The exact prepared stocks are Orris Liquid 9% w/w DEP, Alpha Irone 10% w/w DEP, Ambrettolide 10% w/w DPG and Tonkarome 20% w/w TEC. AIMI/Methyl Ionone Pure is the one user-confirmed bottle identity, not an inferred grade swap. Coriander is seed EO, not leaf oil; vetiver is the owned Indian grade.

All 33 delivered masses are unmeasured. For delivered stock mass m_i and qualified supplied-product mass fraction f_i, product mass = f_i × m_i; product ppm w/w in the raw-stock concentrate = 1,000,000 × f_i × m_i / Σm_i. Neat/as-supplied uses f_i = 1 for the supplied product, not a purity assay. Product ppm is not constituent ppm.

For the four prepared w/w stocks, multiplying raw µL by the mass fraction is only the legacy screening proxy, NOT exact active µL. Do not fill measured-mass or authoritative ppm fields from catalog density. The delivered-mass fields in the canonical design remain null.

Air OAV is modeled vapor ppm divided by air ODT converted to ppm (air ppb / 1,000). Ethanol-solution concentration and ODT must use the same matrix and ppm basis. Do not divide vapor ppm directly by an unconverted ppb number. Every OAV interpretation remains threshold-source- and matrix-dependent.

Use composite constituent screening for supported naturals; unknown or incomplete product composition is not a measured monomolecular substitute. Orris Liquid and Tonkarome product OAVs remain UNKNOWN. Carrot Seed and other natural composites are partial, non-lot-specific models.

The literal ODT dictionary audit found no duplicate keys among the inspected current dictionary, but this does not validate numerical thresholds. Orivone and several low-volatility helper ODTs remain unverified or estimated; Dihydro Beta Ionone's analog-derived threshold is not direct published measurement. Linalool and some natural constituent records disagree across data paths. No pipeline/data/inventory field was edited to force a pass.

## Material selection rationale and survival questions

Each sensory-role material is provisional until its own contribution survives an omission/alternative test. Low modeled OAV does not justify automatic dose inflation; high OAV does not establish quality, safety or intended identity. Where OAV is below one, missing/weak data or a near-source texture hypothesis must be separated from a demonstrated audible layer.

| Material | Hypothesis / purpose | Strongest comparison | Reject or hold condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| Hedione | Floral air around the dense iris centre. | Iso E is a woody contour, not floral air; compare omission at fixed iris. | No repeatable clarity loss, or separate jasmine recognition. |
| Iso E Super | Transparent iris-to-cedar contour. | Compare omission at fixed literal cedar and Hedione. | Anonymous wood dominates, or no transition loss. |
| Vetival | Polished dry wood entering ahead of natural vetiver. | Natural Indian vetiver supplies root, not an assumed equivalent smoother. | No repeatable seam loss, or redundant wood thickening. |
| Benzyl Benzoate | Structural vehicle only; no audible layer credit. | Compare physical compatibility before replacing its solvent-like function. | No claimed odor or projection benefit; solubility remains untested. |
| Hexyl Salicylate | Provisional green/floral cream edge between powder and wood. | Benzyl Salicylate body is separate only if pairwise omission shows a loss. | No repeatable edge change; sub-threshold OAV gives no layer credit. |
| Benzyl Salicylate | Provisional cosmetic-film body. | Hexyl Salicylate is the cream/green edge; compare each and both. | No repeatable body change; do not reinterpret sub-threshold OAV as proven synergy. |
| Vetiver EO (India) | Late earthy root counterline under cedar. | Compare with fixed Vetival to separate root from polish. | Dry wood erases iris or earthy character becomes the subject. |
| Cedarwood oil Virginia | Literal cedar grain linking powder to root return. | Iso E supplies transparency; Cedramber supplies a proposed brighter edge. | Pencil-shaving dominance or no late iris-linked recognition. |
| Cashmeran | Powder-to-wood textile seam. | Kephalis is a darker amber/tobacco pressure; compare omissions and pair. | Textile relation absent or musky wood blankets root. |
| Sandalore | Soft cream-to-grain rounding under iris. | Compare with narrow Ebanol seam, omission and pair; no mandatory DHP material status. | Sandalwood lead or indiscriminate smoothing. |
| Ebanol | Narrow persistent creamy seam against dry wood. | Sandalore is a softer cream hypothesis, not dose-equivalent; compare each and both. | Sandalwood lead or blanket smoothing. |
| Cedramber | Proposed dry diffusive wood edge of the iris contour. | Compare with fixed Iso E and cedar, then alternative edge materials. | Generic amberwood strength without iris recognition; model/ODT uncertain. |
| Kephalis | Dark woody-amber pressure beneath violet and lavender. | Cashmeran gives textile texture; compare separate loss and collision. | Named tobacco/amberwood overrides iris. |
| Ambrettolide | Fruit-to-skin relation at the iris edge. | EB body and Romandolide soft balsamic contour are separately testable roles. | No fruit-to-skin loss or musk blanket. |
| Ethylene Brassylate | Powdered creamy body behind iris. | Ambrettolide fruit and Romandolide late contour are not assumed equivalent. | Body gain without iris clarity, or no omission loss. |
| Romandolide | Soft balsamic musk contour in the late iris/wood context. | Pairwise with EB and Ambrettolide; Habanolide is the strongest separate alternative. | Redundant musk body or loss of root; no unique outward-projection claim. |
| Lavender EO High Altitude | Fine aromatic entry yielding to iris. | Linalyl Acetate adjusts the ester seam; Linalool tests floral continuation. | Lavender remains a separate leading subject. |
| Linalyl Acetate | Soft ester handoff between aromatic entry and fruit/petal. | Compare at fixed lavender and Benzyl Acetate. | No improved handoff, or fruity ester overtakes root. |
| Linalool | Adjustable aromatic-to-floral continuation. | Lavender already contains related chemistry; fixed-lavender omission must establish distinct value. | Redundant opening material or enlarged floral subject. |
| Orris Liquid | Protected whole supplied-product root lead. | Alpha Irone is not an equivalent replacement; no irone assay is inferred. | Root identity obscured; product-composition evidence remains missing. |
| Alpha Isomethyl Ionone (Methyl Ionone Pure) | Protected cosmetic powder body around the root. | Alpha/Beta Ionone and Dihydro Beta Ionone address different violet textures. | Detached violet/powder dominates the root. |
| Alpha Irone | Protected independent root accent. | Neither Beta Ionone nor commercial Orris product is an exact substitute. | Sharp isolated iris accent or no root benefit; lot isomer assay absent. |
| Alpha Ionone | Protected restrained woody-violet movement. | Beta Ionone tests a contrasting violet/fruit edge; no isomer equivalence. | Separate violet flower dominates or no useful movement. |
| Dihydro Beta Ionone | Soft woody-violet pressure behind the violet surface. | Compare against omission and the existing ionone set, not concentration equivalence. | No repeatable inner texture loss; numeric ODT is an analog estimate. |
| Beta Ionone | Contrasting violet-fruit motion against the protected Alpha Ionone. | Compare omission with alpha and methyl-ionone doses fixed; beta-irone is a different molecule. | Violet/berry lead, or no repeatable contrast. |
| Carrot Seed EO | Trace natural earthy-root irregularity. | Orivone is a cleaner camphoraceous contrast; partial composite model is not a lot assay. | Carrot/spice recognizer or no useful irregularity. |
| Orivone | Trace earthy/camphoraceous root contrast. | Carrot Seed tests natural earthy irregularity; compare each and both. | Camphor dominance or root distortion; not described as merely buttery. |
| Tonkarome | Coumarinic fabric warmth under iris. | Isobutavan supplies a separate vanillic seam; product is not pure coumarin. | Pastry/tonka dominates; composition and OAV remain UNKNOWN. |
| Isobutavan | Soft vanillic seam inside the dry-root structure. | Delta Decalactone tests a fatty cream edge, not extra vanilla. | Dessert recognition, or no warm-interior loss on omission. |
| Delta Decalactone | Trace fatty/cream pressure against dry root. | Compare with warm module and omission; not a named peach/coconut accord. | Fruit/milk recognizer or no repeatable creamy-edge loss. |
| Coriander Essential Oil | Fine spicy/aromatic entry contour from owned seed EO. | Lavender supplies aromatic body; no coriander leaf substitution. | Culinary spice recognition or redundant sharpness. |
| Benzyl Acetate | Floral-fruit bridge into ambrettolide and iris. | Linalyl Acetate links lavender; neither is exact Phenethyl Acetate replacement. | Banana/jasmine dominates or no bridge loss. |
| Verdox | Restrained green-fruit glint continuing into wood. | Benzyl Acetate gives floral-fruit softness, not the green wood facet. | Apple/candy becomes the subject or bridge vanishes. |

Orivone is used here specifically as a trace earthy/camphoraceous contrast, consistent with the maker description; its old generic buttery-profile wording is not adopted. [IFF Orivone](https://www.iff.com/scent/ingredients-compendium/orivone/)

### Three-musk exception and pairwise nonredundancy

This candidate deliberately tests three musks, not a default musk cocktail. Ambrettolide owns the fruit-to-skin bridge; Ethylene Brassylate owns powdered body; Romandolide owns a soft balsamic late contour. Romandolide has no established unique outward-projection role.

| Pair | Different jobs to establish | Collision / decisive comparison |
|---|---|---|
| Ambrettolide × Ethylene Brassylate | Fruit-skin relation versus powder body | Both, either alone, neither; reject a blanket that obscures root. |
| Ambrettolide × Romandolide | Fruit transition versus soft late contour | Test late iris recognition, not just musk strength. |
| Ethylene Brassylate × Romandolide | Dense body versus soft balsamic contour | Compare isolated loss; if indistinguishable or no loss, the third musk is not justified. |

Habanolide is the separate strongest alternative to the late-contour job, not an automatic fourth musk. Its role must be compared under matched test conditions. The maker's Romandolide description supports soft musky/balsamic character, not this blend's sillage. [dsm-firmenich Romandolide](https://www.firmenich.com/sites/default/files/uploads/files/ingredients/marketing-sheet/perfumery/ROMANDOLIDE_979031.pdf)

